  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "Four-square representation types have computable contributions determined by permutation multiplicities and sign factors, with verified values for key equality patterns and small n.",
    "plain": "Backfills metadata for the ordering and symmetry distribution of four-square representations. The Lean file defines representation types, enumerates sorted nonnegative quadruples, computes sign/permutation contributions, verifies small totals, and proves closed-form contribution values for several equality patterns.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Lagrange's four-squares theorem counts existence; this slice studies how representations are distributed across symmetries and orderings.",
      "The contribution formula separates permutation multiplicities from sign choices, making the combinatorics inspectable.",
      "Orbit-stabilizer checks explain the numerical contribution factors that appear in four-square counts."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "`totalFromEnum` computes representation counts from enumerated type contributions.",
      "`totalFromEnum_1` through later concrete checks verify small values such as n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 14, 15, and 16.",
      "`trivialType_contribution`, `allEqualType_contribution`, `threeEqualType_contribution`, `twoPairType_contribution`, and related theorems prove closed-form contributions for equality patterns.",
      "`allDistinctType_contribution` proves the all-distinct contribution is 384, with `symmetry_ratio_eq_factorial` connecting the ratio to 4!.",
      "`orbit_stabilizer_trivial` and companion theorems record stabilizer-size ratios for several type families.",
      "The exact `LagrangeFourSquaresOQ02.lean` file is recorded with 0 axioms and 0 sorries; sibling four-squares files include separate axioms."
    ],
    "open": [
      "The metadata is scoped to the clean OQ02 ordering/symmetry file and does not claim the axiom-dependent base Lagrange proof."
    ],
    "goal": "Expose the clean four-squares ordering-distribution theorem evidence while clearly separating sibling-file assumptions."
  },
